WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.027 --> 00:00:01.530
- So, where did this idea for the

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:02.760
Kids Tonight Show come from?

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.038
- We grew up with shows
where kids were hosts,

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.350
Mickey Mouse Club

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.760
or even the Electric Company or Zoom

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.555
or whatever, the other
zoom and 3, 2, 1 Contact.

00:00:11.555 --> 00:00:12.946
- The O.G.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:13.779
- Yeah.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:14.612
But right now, kids,

00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:15.560
I have two little girls,

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.850
and they don't really have a show

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.190
where kids are hosts of things.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:19.023
- Yeah.

00:00:19.023 --> 00:00:19.856
And so I go,

00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:20.689
we should do that.

00:00:20.689 --> 00:00:22.051
Then I'm like, oh wait, I am a host.

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:22.884
We should have the Kids Tonight Show.

00:00:22.884 --> 00:00:25.960
So we have four amazing
kids. They couldn't be cuter.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.302
And they're a little 15
minute segments of the show.

00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:31.030
So it's good bite size.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.930
Perfect for parents who were like, okay,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.810
one show and then you're
going to bed let's go.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.220
- Amen. Yeah, I know that drill.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.660
- You know? So we just figured,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.230
we'd start it up. It's on Peacock.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.880
And they interview celebrities.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.500
They interview other kids.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.640
There's music, it's messy,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.473
It's fun.

00:00:45.473 --> 00:00:47.430
It's just 15 minutes of joy

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.184
and pure fun family entertainment.

00:00:49.184 --> 00:00:50.017
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:51.423
- And yeah, we lucked out.

00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:52.990
It was a great, great, great, great show.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:53.823
Top to bottom.

00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:55.219
- [Kelly] It's such a brilliant idea.

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:56.240
- Lucky.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.810
- Well, so the book you wrote,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.160
which my kids love by the way.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.679
It's your Christmas one.

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:01.512
- Oh.

00:01:01.512 --> 00:01:02.345
- And it's a special now. Yeah.

00:01:02.345 --> 00:01:04.620
- My daughter came home from school once,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.360
and she was going to
her grandmother's house

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.193
for the weekend.

00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:09.026
She was like,

00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:10.263
"How many more sleeps till..."

00:01:10.263 --> 00:01:11.550
shouldn't even have a lisp,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.383
but I just added that.

00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:13.573
- [Kelly] I was like,
wait, what's happening?

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:14.406
- Nah, nah, yeah she's cute.

00:01:14.406 --> 00:01:15.239
She has a little lisp.

00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:16.072
She's like, "How many more sleeps

00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:17.180
till Grandma's house?"

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.160
How many more sleeps?

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.010
I never heard that.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.346
- [Kelly] Oh my God. My
kids say that all the time.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:21.560
That's why they love your book.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.050
- [Jimmy] So I wrote the book

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.580
Five More Sleeps Till Christmas,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:25.767
about a kid excited about Christmas.

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:29.920
And then it became now a animated show.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:30.876
So it's a cartoon

00:01:30.876 --> 00:01:31.709
- [Kelly] That's so cool

00:01:31.709 --> 00:01:32.542
- like Charlie Brown

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:33.375
- or whatever.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:34.208
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:34.208 --> 00:01:36.274
- It's crazy. It airs the
day after Thanksgiving.

00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:37.660
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.500
- So the 26th, it air's on
NBC and we made it and it's

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.350
like, kind of Claymation stop motion,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.670
but also painted, the
animation is different

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.580
than anything you've seen.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:45.582
It's about a boy and his dog
and his mom, and they get

00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:49.760
a stranger is now involved
with their Christmas

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.970
and hopefully it doesn't
mess up any of his plans.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:52.803
- Okay.

00:01:52.803 --> 00:01:53.636
- Yeah. It's cute.

00:01:53.636 --> 00:01:54.907
- Not stranger danger.
Cause that's kind of what--

00:01:54.907 --> 00:01:56.371
- [Jimmy] No! Oh no, no.

00:01:56.371 --> 00:01:58.210
- [Kelly] I'm just kidding.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.930
My kids love, my kids love your book.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.630
They love reading, we read
every night, so it's awesome,

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.814
cause there's not that many,

00:02:02.814 --> 00:02:04.380
Christmas stories like that,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.213
to be able to read to them. So.

00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:06.380
- But I love your book too.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.920
- [Kelly] Oh well, thank you.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:08.753
- [Jimmy] Uh yeah.

00:02:08.753 --> 00:02:09.586
- [Kelly] Well thank you.

00:02:09.586 --> 00:02:11.740
Well, we're talking kids and
it's throwback Thursdays.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.840
So how about let's look at you as a kid.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.280
Cause I thought that'd be
fun. So here's this photo.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.553
(Kelly laughing)
(Audience swoons)

00:02:18.553 --> 00:02:19.680
- [Jimmy] Oh God. Oh my God.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.900
Look at the acid wash Jean.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:21.733
I don't even know

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:22.566
if that's acid wash.

00:02:22.566 --> 00:02:23.403
- [Kelly] And it's like Reboks?

00:02:23.403 --> 00:02:25.156
I don't know, it's awesome!

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:26.490
- [Jimmy] Yeah, something, yeah.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.810
Something was on the discount rack.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.710
I don't know all the whole outfit, but.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.176
- We're all the ladies on you?

00:02:31.176 --> 00:02:36.070
- [Kelly] I'm just kidding.
We're you just like --

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.264
- You know what happened?

00:02:37.264 --> 00:02:38.900
Jimmy we found the perfect background,

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.903
this weird corner somewhere.

00:02:42.126 --> 00:02:44.450
- With all the wood.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.506
- Please take that photo off.

00:02:46.506 --> 00:02:48.020
It was in high school

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.190
and I couldn't make the football team

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.023
or the baseball team.

00:02:51.023 --> 00:02:52.312
I just wasn't that athletic.

00:02:52.312 --> 00:02:54.570
but I wanted to play for a team.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.280
And I remember someone
came on the announcement,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.123
the loudspeaker, and if
anyone wants to play golf,

00:03:00.123 --> 00:03:03.300
anyone, we would love
you for the golf team.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.890
Or else we have to forfeit the
team. We won't have a team.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.984
- So that's how you made a team?

00:03:07.984 --> 00:03:09.906
(audience laughs)

00:03:09.906 --> 00:03:12.591
- So that's how, that's how.
If I didn't make the team

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:13.950
there was no team.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:14.783
- [Kelly] We literally can't!

00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:16.630
- So I literally made the team.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:17.463
That's right!

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:18.450
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.170
- Hey. You got there. You got there.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.407
- [Jimmy] That's how I got in.

00:03:22.407 --> 00:03:23.437
And there would be no team.

00:03:23.437 --> 00:03:24.630
- [Kelly] It was no audition.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.910
- And I didn't even know how to golf.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.640
And I remember as a kid in a week,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.570
we used to grow tomatoes in our backyard.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.860
And there were all these sticks

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.410
in the garden

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:34.810
to keep the tomatoes from falling over.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.010
And one of them had like
a thing on the end of it.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.500
And I said to my
grandfather, what is this?

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.010
And he goes, "It's a golf club.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:40.900
You don't know what golfing...?"

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.134
- And yet, you made the
golf team! That's amazing!

00:03:45.134 --> 00:03:47.125
- You think, totally ridiculous.

00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:47.958
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:03:47.958 --> 00:03:48.791
- I'm not good. Never, never

00:03:48.791 --> 00:03:50.541
never became good, but I still play it.

00:03:50.541 --> 00:03:51.420
And my grandpa's like,
"It's a good investment.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:52.700
That's a good sport to
get into because you can

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.070
play it for the rest of your life."

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:55.770
- Well, I told you I'm into like top golf.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:56.916
That's the kind of golf I'm into.

00:03:56.916 --> 00:03:57.749
- [Jimmy] Yes.

00:03:57.749 --> 00:03:59.175
- You've never been and
you're going to go with me.

00:03:59.175 --> 00:04:00.008
- I would totally go with you.

00:04:00.008 --> 00:04:00.841
- Okay, it'd be fun.

00:04:00.841 --> 00:04:01.970
- I would love to do it.
I haven't done it yet.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.300
- Yeah it's really, really fun.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.090
Anyway, you drink, you
eat, you, you're married.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.780
Do you remember your
very first comedy gig?

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.082
And were you terrified?

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:11.030
- I mean, yeah.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:13.230
I used to do jokes around the
house. My family, you know,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.662
they would give me 50 cents or a dollar.

00:04:15.662 --> 00:04:16.681
- [Kelly] Really?

00:04:16.681 --> 00:04:18.150
- And just go do Rodney
Dangerfield or something.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.471
And I be like, oh, tell
you all right, okay.

00:04:20.471 --> 00:04:23.031
My wife's cooking so bad. I mean, uh,

00:04:23.031 --> 00:04:25.253
since when does toast have bones?

00:04:26.413 --> 00:04:29.080
And so I do that, but my first big gig,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.630
my mom heard about an
impression contest on the radio.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.090
And she said, "Jimmy,

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.280
I know you do all these voices

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.120
in your in your bedroom, I can hear you."

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:38.953
- [Kelly] Your--

00:04:41.645 --> 00:04:42.727
There's so many jokes!

00:04:42.727 --> 00:04:44.890
- Yeah, how thin are these walls? Yeah.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.098
What else you hear in
there mom? Uh, anyways.

00:04:46.098 --> 00:04:49.820
So she goes, but why don't
you enter this contest

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.790
as to who can do the best
impression in the Hudson Valley?

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.474
And I went to this club
called Bananas in Poughkeepsie

00:04:55.474 --> 00:04:58.150
and I was 17, I think I want to say?

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.640
I was a senior in high school.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.790
And I went and I did all
these voices for a spokesman,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.720
for a troll doll someone gave me

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:05.712
for graduating.

00:05:05.712 --> 00:05:07.280
I don't know what to do with this doll.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.013
So I just made it act out of it.

00:05:09.013 --> 00:05:09.846
So I'd be like,

00:05:09.846 --> 00:05:12.540
Uh, who wants to be a
spokesman for the troll doll?

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:13.373
First up,

00:05:13.373 --> 00:05:16.977
uh, I would do like a Adam
Sandler and I'd be like,

00:05:16.977 --> 00:05:20.139
(speaking gibberish)

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:21.870
- [Kelly] Oh my god!

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.740
- Or John Travolta, I'm
like, who does his hair?

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.217
this thing is like so crazy,

00:05:25.217 --> 00:05:28.570
what kind of troll doll is
this?? It's crazy hair right?

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.240
Next up, Seinfeld.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.840
Oh who plays with these dolls?

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.993
The arms and legs don't
move! This isn't a doll!

00:05:34.993 --> 00:05:35.826
[Jimmy] You know,

00:05:35.826 --> 00:05:37.762
And I ended up, I ended up winning.

00:05:37.762 --> 00:05:39.972
I won first place. I
won a thousand dollars.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:40.805
- [Kelly] Oh my god!

00:05:40.805 --> 00:05:42.014
(audience applauding)

00:05:42.014 --> 00:05:45.105
- A thousand dollars when
you're 17 is like the lottery.

00:05:45.105 --> 00:05:46.880
- And then it got the
gigs that my dad would

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:47.713
drive me to the gigs.

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:49.588
I would host weekend shows--

00:05:49.588 --> 00:05:50.610
- [Kelly] That's so cool and supportive.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.725
- They were, I mean,

00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:52.558
(audience laughs)

00:05:52.558 --> 00:05:55.760
well I mean they were,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.673
but, they were like, Jimmy, this is great.

00:05:58.673 --> 00:06:01.500
I think, you know, you
should go to college

00:06:02.484 --> 00:06:03.510
and think about something else.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:04.800
You know, just have a backup.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:05.908
They made me take the postal exam

00:06:05.908 --> 00:06:06.741
- Okay.

00:06:06.741 --> 00:06:07.574
- And I could've worked

00:06:07.574 --> 00:06:09.210
for the us postal service, easy.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.043
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:06:10.043 --> 00:06:10.876
- Yeah, instead --

00:06:10.876 --> 00:06:11.860
- [Kelly] So you had a backup plan.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.547
- [Jimmy] I had --
- [Kelly] Yeah, for when --

00:06:13.547 --> 00:06:14.608
- [Jimmy] Three back up plans.

00:06:14.608 --> 00:06:17.511
And so I ended up
luckily being a comedian.

00:06:17.511 --> 00:06:20.600
I moved out to LA to do acting classes,

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.120
which you can tell how good of an actor

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.020
have you seen my movies?

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:24.010
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:25.029
- You are!

00:06:25.029 --> 00:06:25.862
- No one has, no.

00:06:25.862 --> 00:06:29.570
- I find that comedians
are -- y'all can go deeper

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:30.670
than anyone else to me,

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:32.505
and even think of your favorite actors.

00:06:32.505 --> 00:06:34.300
Like one of mine is Meryl.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.170
Okay. So funny, her
comedic timing is genius.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.290
She's so funny. Like you have to be witty.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:40.970
You have to be of a certain
level of intelligence

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:41.803
to be that kind of witty

00:06:41.803 --> 00:06:43.290
and that kind of great actor, I think.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.123
- Well...

00:06:44.123 --> 00:06:46.882
(audience laughs)

00:06:46.882 --> 00:06:48.465
- What's happening?

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.009
- I appreciate it. I appreciate it.

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:54.172
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:06:54.172 --> 00:06:57.055
And I appreciate it. I promised
myself I wouldn't do this.

00:06:57.055 --> 00:07:00.795
I appreciate everything that
you've ever done for me.

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:02.051
I love you, Kelly.

00:07:02.051 --> 00:07:04.152
- So maybe you should
have been a postal worker.

00:07:04.152 --> 00:07:05.453
- Okay, yeah.

